      "✅one line|BEATS 100% Runtime||Explanation✅ vishnoi29 5369 33364 Oct 02, 2023 C++ Java Python3 C# 6+ Intution \n we have to count the occurrence of the same elements \nwith  A[i] == A[j]  and  i < j \n Approach \n \n We will  intiliaze ans with 0  and an empty  unordered map  to store the occurrence of the element \n For each element in the given array: \n Here there will be 2 cases\n \n if element/number is  present in the map  that means for example at any time in unordered map we saw count of num(element) 1 is 2 thats means currunt element can form 2 pair with previous 1, so at that time  we will add this count in answer  and also  increase the count of the element in out map \n If element/number is  not present in the map , it means this is the first time we're seeing this number, so we  initialize its count to 1. \n \n \n At last we will return our answer \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity :  O(N) \n Space complexity :  O(N) \n \n Codes \n C++_one_line C++ Java Python C# Rust Ruby JavaScript Go PHP function   numIdenticalPairs ( A )   { \n      let  ans  =   0 ; \n      const  cnt  =   { } ; \n     \n      for   ( let  x  of   A )   { \n         ans  +=  cnt [ x ]   ||   0 ; \n         cnt [ x ]   =   ( cnt [ x ]   ||   0 )   +   1 ; \n      } \n     \n      return  ans ; \n } \n \n \n If you really found my solution helpful  please upvote it , as it motivates me to post such kind of codes. \n Let me know in comment if i can do better. \nLet's Connect on  LINKDIN \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/b0bf2fa1-1680-41fc-be3f-3ba1c8745505_1675216604.7695017.jpeg\" alt=\"upvote.jfif\"> Comments (17) vishnoi29 Oct 02, 2023 Simple code>>>>One line \nSimplicity in code design is the art of achieving elegance and clarity, while one-liners are the poetry of concise expression. 73 2 eddyleet Oct 03, 2023 bro your soution is outstanding 4 Frost12392 Feb 20, 2024 You are amazing, I love you 3 thanksmom Oct 03, 2023 class Solution:     def numIdenticalPairs(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:         return sum([math.comb(n, 2) for n in collections.Counter(nums).values()]) \n How would this ever beat 100% when it Counter() over all values, for each of them math.comb, and then sum. 2 parthdharmale008 Oct 02, 2023 Better explained  here 2 Tyrex_19 Oct 02, 2023 Here is a C++ map-based solution, with an animation attached  here \nIf you prefer video content, you can watch it here. \nYour feedback will be gladly appreciate \n Read more 1 roshanbro77 May 11, 2024 Space complexity should be O(1) because it is taking constant space of an array of length 101 which is independent of size of input array. right ?? 0 euphori_A Mar 22, 2024 isn't the time comp;exity is :  NlogN? \nbecause as far i know the insertion operation in a map takes logN times and the for the size of the vector it will take N*logN .innit? 0 1 raseldc Feb 14, 2024 (Y) 0 user8452a Feb 02, 2024 Bro how are you getitng the magic number 101 ? 0 1",
      "✅98.44%🔥Easy Solution🔥Array & Math🔥 MrAke 5916 10153 Oct 02, 2023 C++ Java Python Python3 6+ Problem \n The problem you described is a classic counting problem that asks you to find the number of \"good pairs\" in an array of integers. A good pair is defined as a pair of indices (i, j) where i < j and the elements at those indices in the array are equal (nums[i] == nums[j]). \n \n Solution \n The given solution uses a nested loop to iterate through all possible pairs of indices (i, j) where i < j. For each pair, it checks whether the elements at those indices are equal, and if they are, it increments the count variable. Finally, it returns the count, which represents the number of good pairs in the array. \n 1. Initialize a variable count to 0. This variable will be used to keep track of the number of good pairs. \n 2. Use a for loop to iterate through each index i in the array nums. This loop represents the first element of a potential pair. \n 3. Within the outer loop, use another for loop to iterate through each index j in the array nums, where j is greater than i. This loop represents the second element of the pair and ensures that i < j. \n 4. Inside the inner loop, check if nums[i] is equal to nums[j]. If they are equal, it means you've found a good pair, so increment the count variable by 1. \n 5. Continue this process, checking all possible pairs in the array. \n 6. After both loops have finished, the count variable will hold the total number of good pairs. \n 7. Finally, return the count as the result. \n This solution has a time complexity of O(n^2) because it uses nested loops to compare all possible pairs of elements in the array. For larger arrays, this can be inefficient, so there are more optimized approaches to solving this problem with a time complexity of O(n). \n \n Code \n Python C# C++ C Java JavaScript var   numIdenticalPairs   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  count  =   0 ; \n     \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  nums . length ;  i ++ )   { \n          for   ( let  j  =  i  +   1 ;  j  <  nums . length ;  j ++ )   { \n              if   ( nums [ i ]   ===  nums [ j ] )   { \n                 count ++ ; \n              } \n          } \n      } \n     \n      return  count ; \n } ; \n \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/fc3ef886-5fc5-4b2d-aa6c-41e84a7c65dc_1696297073.543429.png\" alt=\"9c6f9412-c860-47d6-9a2e-7fcf37ff3321_1686334926.180891.png\"> Comments (9) SergeyZhirkov Oct 02, 2023 Please Upvote my solution, if you find it helpful :) \nEasy 1 line solution (Java Stream) \n https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-good-pairs/solutions/4122119/easy-1-line-solution-java-stream/ 2 Tyrex_19 Oct 02, 2023 Here is a C++ map-based solution, with an animation attached  here \nIf you prefer video content, you can watch it here. \nYour feedback will be gladly appreciate \n Read more 1 thanksmom Oct 03, 2023 This is a poor solution and should fail any interview -- O(n^2).  You only need to go through the array one time O(n). 1 1 Mrgupta Oct 03, 2023 BOMBASTIC SIDE EYES SOLUtion :-  https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-good-pairs/discuss/4126355/orBEATS-45.5-RuntimeororExplanationor-BOMB-SOLUTION 0 agr__rachit Oct 03, 2023 Join this group for coding related discussions:- \n https://chat.whatsapp.com/Ly8m0CLglOWFWtzp1XIWqC 0 idiocracyisadocumentary Oct 03, 2023 Thank you for the solution. \nFor you Java code solution, it is missing the if statement \nif (i < j) { \ncount++; \n} 0 1 Vala_Dhruv Oct 02, 2023 Easy solution with 2 Apporoaches:  https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-good-pairs/solutions/4122895/easy-sol-t-c-o-n/ 0 parthdharmale008 Oct 02, 2023 Explained brute force to optimal  here 0 shahid_2048 Oct 02, 2023 Read more 0",
      "Clean JavaScript Solution shimphillip 254 5811 Oct 26, 2020 JavaScript // time O(N^2) space O(1) \n   var   numIdenticalPairs   =   function ( nums )   { \n       let  count  =   0 \n     \n       for ( let  i = 0 ;  i < nums . length ;  i ++ )   { \n           for ( let  j = i + 1 ;  j < nums . length ;  j ++ )   { \n               if ( nums [ i ]   ===  nums [ j ] )   { \n                  count ++ \n               } \n           } \n       } \n     \n       return  count\n   } ; \n // time O(N) space O(N) \n var   numIdenticalPairs   =   function ( nums )   { \n      const  map  =   { } \n      let  count  =   0 \n     \n      for   ( const   number   of  nums )   { \n          if   ( map [ number ] )   { \n             count  +=  map [ number ] ; \n             map [ number ]   +=   1 ; \n          }   else   { \n             map [ number ]   =   1 ; \n          } \n      } \n      return  count\n } ; Comments (5) Minato235 Sep 05, 2022 this is My solution \n \nvar numIdenticalPairs = function(nums) { \nvar count=0; \nvar i=0; \nnums.sort(function(a,b){ \nreturn a-b; \n}) \n// [ 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3 ] \n \n for(var j=1;j<nums.length;j++){ \nif(nums[i]===nums[j]){ \ncount+=j-i; \n}else{ \ni=j; \n} \n} \nreturn count; \n}; Read more 1 ReluctantFoamRoller May 13, 2021 In your map solution, can you explain the idea behind these 2 lines? \n count += map[number]; \nmap[number] += 1; 1 3 ScarletPumpernickel Sep 06, 2023 You can shave one cycle of your first solution: i<nums.length - 1 (leave space for j). 0 asura1990 Apr 12, 2021 I always look for your solutions and compare them to mine, keep it up thanks 0 user4278X Feb 15, 2021 Thanks you shimphillip , am learning js algo with your solutions, :D 0",
      "✅ 95.45% Easy Count vanAmsen 11200 8155 Oct 02, 2023 C++ Java Python3 C# 5+ Intuition \n When confronted with the problem of identifying pairs of identical numbers, it's natural to consider tracking the occurrences of each number. By knowing how many times a number has appeared before, we can infer how many pairs can be made with this number. \n Live Coding & Explain \n \n Approach \n \n \n Initialization : \n \n We create a dictionary,  count , to maintain the number of occurrences of each number. \n We also maintain a  result  variable initialized to 0 to count the number of good pairs. \n \n \n \n Traversing the List : \n \n For each  num  in the list  nums :\n \n If  num  is already in the dictionary  count , it means we've seen this number before.\n \n Every time we see a repeated number, it can form a pair with its previous occurrences. So, if a number has appeared  k  times before, it can form  k  pairs with this occurrence. We increase the  result  by the count of this number ( count[num] ). \n We then increase the count of this number in the dictionary. \n \n \n If  num  is not in the dictionary, it means this is the first time we're seeing this number, so we initialize its count to 1. \n \n \n \n \n \n Return Result : \n \n Finally, we return the  result , which represents the number of good pairs. \n \n \n \n Why It Works \n This logic works because each time we encounter a repeated number, we know it can form pairs with its previous occurrences. By maintaining a count of each number's occurrences, we can quickly determine the number of pairs it can form without having to explicitly enumerate them. \n What We Learned \n This problem teaches us the power of hashing and its utility in efficiently tracking occurrences or frequencies. By leveraging hash maps (dictionaries in Python), we can achieve solutions that are more efficient than naive approaches, such as nested loops. \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity :  O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n We traverse the list once, and dictionary operations (checking existence, updating value) are generally  O(1)O(1) O ( 1 )  on average. \n \n \n Space complexity :  O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n In the worst case, every number in the list is unique, leading to a dictionary size that is proportional to the size of the list. \n \n \n \n Code \n Python C++ Java C# Rust Go JavaScript PHP var   numIdenticalPairs   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  count  =   { } ; \n      let  result  =   0 ; \n \n      for   ( let  num  of  nums )   { \n          if   ( num  in  count )   { \n             result  +=  count [ num ] ; \n             count [ num ] ++ ; \n          }   else   { \n             count [ num ]   =   1 ; \n          } \n      } \n \n      return  result ; \n } ; \n Performance \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n Language Execution Time (ms) Memory (MB) Rust 0 2.1 Java 0 40.6 Go 1 2 C++ 3 7.6 PHP 11 19 Python3 33 16.2 JavaScript 49 41.5 C# 60 38.3 \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/ac230a79-27a3-4222-9d5b-039817dbae2b_1696295253.9515915.png\" alt=\"vn.png\"> Comments (7) vanAmsen Oct 02, 2023 Every line of code you write brings an idea to life. Embrace the challenge, for with each task, you're not just solving problems – you're crafting possibilities. 10 sergei99 Oct 03, 2023 Amazing. Summing of frequencies within the same loop as accumulating them seems to be much faster than a separate run calculating  k * (k - 1) / 2 . But I would have never thought of using a hash table for frequencies. 0 parthdharmale008 Oct 02, 2023 Explained brute force to optimal  here 0 shahid_2048 Oct 02, 2023 Read more 0 Tyrex_19 Oct 02, 2023 Here is a C++ map-based solution, with an animation attached  here \nIf you prefer video content, you can watch it here. \nYour feedback will be gladly appreciate \n Read more 0 Vansh_Rana_611 Oct 02, 2023 hey brother good morning thanks for your intuition and explanation. 0 SergeyZhirkov Oct 02, 2023 Please Upvote my solution, if you find it helpful :) \nEasy 1 line solution (Java Stream) \n https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-good-pairs/solutions/4122119/easy-1-line-solution-java-stream/ -1",
      "【Video】How we think about a solution - Python, JavaScript, Java, C++ niits 10383 1418 Oct 02, 2023 C++ Java Python3 JavaScript Welcome to my post! This post starts with \"How we think about a solution\". In other words, that is my thought process to solve the question. This post explains how I get to my solution instead of just posting solution codes or out of blue algorithms. I hope it is helpful for someone. \n Intuition \n Using HashMap \n \n Solution Video \n ⭐️⭐️ Don't forget to subscribe to my channel! ⭐️⭐️ \n \n ■ Timeline of the video \n 0:00  Read the question of Number of Good Pairs \n 0:17  How we think about a solution \n 1:54  Demonstrate solution with an example. \n 4:48  Coding \n 6:05  Time Complexity and Space Complexity \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n Subscribers: 2,582 \nMy initial goal is 10,000 \nThank you for your support! \n \n Approach \n How we think about a solution \n I think the simplest way to solve this question is brute force like this. \n class   Solution : \n     def  numIdenticalPairs ( self ,  nums :   List [ int ] )   ->   int : \n         count  =   0 \n          for  i in  range ( len ( nums ) ) : \n              for  j in  range ( i  +   1 ,   len ( nums ) ) : \n                  if  nums [ i ]   ==  nums [ j ] : \n                     count  +=   1 \n         \n          return  count \n Space compelxity is  O(1)O(1) O ( 1 )  but time complexity is  O(n2)O(n^2) O ( n 2 ) . Actually this brute force passed all test cases and got success. \n In real interview,  O(n2)O(n^2) O ( n 2 )  is not good for the most cases. Can we improve time complexity? That's how I started thinking  O(n)O(n) O ( n )  time. \n To improve time,  I thought I need to keep number of occurrence of each previous number. In that case, what data structure do you use? \n \n ⭐️ Points \n My answer is to use  HashMap  to keep number of occurrence of each previous number. Key is each number and value is the cumulative numbers of each value that I found before. \n \n Why do we need cumulative numbers of each value? \n \n That's because when we find new same number in later index, we are sure we can create number of occurrence of the same number pairs with the number we found this time. \n \n Let's think about a simple example. \n Input: [1,1,1]\n Output: 3 \n From  index 0 , we can create two pairs  (0, 1)  and  (0, 2)  and from  index 1 , we can create one pair  (1, 2) . That's why we should return  3 (pairs). \n Let's see inside of  HashMap \n We are now  index 0 \n If we have the same key in  HashMap , calculate  count (return value) with  cumulative numbers of current value so far \n count  =   0 ,  because no  data   in  HashMap \n then add  current value  as a key and  cumulative numbers of current value so far + 1  as a value \n HashMap = {1: 1} \n +1  is coming from a current value, because we found it this time. \n At  index 1 , we have  1  in  HashMap , so calculate  count  with  cumulative numbers of current value so far . \n count  =   1 ,  coming  from   HashMap   =   { 1 :   1 } \n This indicates  (1, 1)  coming from  index 0  and  index 1 \n then add  current value  as a key and  cumulative numbers of current value so far + 1  as a value \n HashMap = {1: 2} \n At  index 2 , we have  1  in  HashMap , so calculate  count  with  cumulative numbers of current value so far . \n count  =   3 ,  coming  from   HashMap   =   { 1 :   2 } \n This indicates  (1, 1)  coming from  index 0  and  index 2  and  (1, 1)  coming from  index 1  and  index 2 \n Output: 3 \n Although I was thinking of providing more examples, I believe this should be sufficient for understanding. Let's proceed to review a real algorithm. \n Algorithm Overview: \n \n Create a dictionary  pairs  to store the counts of occurrences of each number. \n Initialize a variable  count  to 0 to keep track of the number of good pairs. \n Iterate through the input array  nums . \n For each element in  nums , check if it's already in the  pairs  dictionary. \n If the element is in  pairs , update the  count  by adding the current count of that element from the  pairs  dictionary. \n Update the count of the current element in the  pairs  dictionary. \n Return the total count of good pairs. \n \n Detailed Explanation: \n \n Create an empty dictionary  pairs  to keep track of the counts of each number. \n Initialize a variable  count  to 0, which will store the count of good pairs. \n Iterate through the input array  nums  using a  for  loop, using the loop variable  i  to index the elements. \n Inside the loop, check if the current element  nums[i]  is already a key in the  pairs  dictionary. \n If  nums[i]  is a key in  pairs , increment the  count  by the value associated with  nums[i]  in the  pairs  dictionary. This is because for every occurrence of  nums[i] , it forms a good pair with each previous occurrence. \n Update the count of occurrences of the current element  nums[i]  in the  pairs  dictionary using  pairs.get(nums[i], 0) + 1 . If  nums[i]  is not a key in  pairs , this sets its count to 1. If it's already a key, it increments its count. \n After processing all elements in  nums , return the total count of good pairs, which is stored in the  count  variable. \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity:  O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Space complexity:  O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \nFor  HashMap . It depends on size of input array. \n \n \n Python JavaScript Java C++  \n var   numIdenticalPairs   =   function ( nums )   { \n      const  pairs  =   { } ; \n      let  count  =   0 ; \n \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  nums . length ;  i ++ )   { \n          if   ( nums [ i ]   in  pairs )   { \n             count  +=  pairs [ nums [ i ] ] ; \n          } \n         \n         pairs [ nums [ i ] ]   =   ( pairs [ nums [ i ] ]   ||   0 )   +   1 ; \n      } \n \n      return  count ;     \n } ; \n \n Thank you for reading my post. \n⭐️ Please upvote it and don't forget to subscribe to my channel! \n My next post for daily coding challenge on Oct 4, 2023 \n https://leetcode.com/problems/design-hashmap/solutions/4130553/video-how-we-think-about-a-solution-python-javascript-java-c/ Comments (6) niits Oct 02, 2023 ⭐️⭐️ Don't forget to subscribe to my channel! ⭐️⭐️ \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n Subscribers: 2,582 \nMy initial goal is 10,000 \nThank you for your support! 7 agr__rachit Oct 03, 2023 Join this group to solve your doubts and discuss different methods:- \n https://chat.whatsapp.com/Ly8m0CLglOWFWtzp1XIWqC 0 kikiana Oct 03, 2023 I like your post! 0 parthdharmale008 Oct 02, 2023 Explained brute force to optimal  here 0 Tyrex_19 Oct 02, 2023 Here is a C++ map-based solution, with an animation attached  here \nIf you prefer video content, you can watch it here. \nYour feedback will be gladly appreciate \n Read more 0 SergeyZhirkov Oct 02, 2023 Please Upvote my solution, if you find it helpful :) \nEasy 1 line solution (Java Stream) \n https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-good-pairs/solutions/4122119/easy-1-line-solution-java-stream/ 0"
